00:04:10.433 --> 00:04:11.533
Okay, to begin

00:04:11.666 --> 00:04:14.656
we want to talk about
what a "typical" NGS workflow is.

00:04:14.766 --> 00:04:16.366
"Typical” is in quotes
for a reason

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.033
because there really isn’t
a typical NGS workflow.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.533
In general though

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.233
it follows this workflow
that we have on the slide.

00:04:24.566 --> 00:04:29.166
You start with cDNA synthesis
for RNA seek applications

00:04:29.633 --> 00:04:31.566
and then continuing from there

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.300
you have your traditional
Library Construction desk

00:04:34.466 --> 00:04:39.400
which will include end repair,
A-tailing, ligation of the adaptors

00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:43.133
and subsequent cleanups
after each of those steps.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.366
PCR Enrichment
is optional for certain workflows

00:04:46.533 --> 00:04:49.133
and depending on the workflow

00:04:49.333 --> 00:04:54.766
Target Capture is also an optional
part of an NGS workflow.

00:04:55.866 --> 00:04:59.000
Following the traditional
library prep construction

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:03.766
you will continue on
to do Library QC Quantization

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.066
and Sequencing Preparation

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.500
and the actual
Sequencing run itself.

00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:19.800
So although most NGS workflows
follow this consistent path,

00:05:19.966 --> 00:05:23.400
the specific workflow
can vary greatly for each application.

00:05:23.666 --> 00:05:26.766
They can range from single day,
short and simple protocols,

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.733
to multi-day,
highly complex protocols.

00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:33.030
Even though
the workflows can vary greatly

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:37.166
they all share common elements,
such as reagent and enzyme transfers,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.266
sample transfers,
bead-based sample cleanup,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.866
and/or size selection,

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.400
as well as thermal cycling
and incubation reactions.

00:05:46.566 --> 00:05:49.833
So we focus on the modularity
of those common elements

00:05:50.166 --> 00:05:53.290
and optimize those and then
construct them in different ways

00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:00.833
to basically assemble the workflows
for each individual application.

00:06:04.066 --> 00:06:09.833
Here we have some workflow examples
of a simple NGS workflow

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.333
for the Rubicon Genomics
ThruPlex® Plasma-seq chemistry.

00:06:13.733 --> 00:06:17.100
The protocol takes approximately
3.5 hours to complete.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.376
It’s performed in a single plate
for all the reactions

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:23.033
and there is a limited number
of start and stop points.

00:06:23.166 --> 00:06:25.400
So it’s a relatively simple
manual process

00:06:25.533 --> 00:06:29.715
and also
a relatively simple application

00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:31.333
on the automation as well.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.233
The first column here, that’s
basically your Library Construction

00:06:36.433 --> 00:06:39.466
and the second column
is an optional cleanup

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.100
following
your Library Construction.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.100
The next example gets into more,
I guess, the “typical” workflow

00:06:47.966 --> 00:06:49.766
as far as complexity is concerned.

00:06:49.933 --> 00:06:54.566
And this example is for the Illumina
TruSeq Nano DNA chemistry.

00:06:54.766 --> 00:06:56.766
It’s approximately a 1-day protocol.

00:06:56.966 --> 00:07:00.266
It involves multiple reactions
and cleanup plates

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.433
and has multiple
start and stop points.

00:07:06.966 --> 00:07:10.033
And if you continue on
to a difficult

00:07:10.366 --> 00:07:12.466
or highly complex method example

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.800
this is an example of the Illumina
TruSeq RNA Access method

00:07:16.566 --> 00:07:19.133
which is approximately
a 3-day protocol.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.231
It includes
Target Capture following

00:07:21.333 --> 00:07:23.733
Library Construction
or Adaptor Ligation.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.066
And it has an increasingly larger
number of labware consumables

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.666
due to the overall length
of the process

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.733
and it’s highly complex
with numerous start and stop points.

00:07:36.266 --> 00:07:40.333
So again, all of these workflows
follow a series of reaction setups,

00:07:41.433 --> 00:07:43.866
incubations, cleanups, etc.

00:07:44.433 --> 00:07:46.733
that’s just
the length of the application

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:51.411
and the complexity of how
those steps are pieced together

00:07:51.533 --> 00:07:53.600
to create the overall workflow.

00:08:04.833 --> 00:08:09.633
So if you’re already processing
NGS samples by hand

00:08:11.166 --> 00:08:15.666
you know the challenges
of the NGS workflow.

00:08:16.466 --> 00:08:17.745
So some of those challenges

00:08:17.866 --> 00:08:20.516
are significant amount
of manual pipetting steps.

00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:22.751
So with a significant
amount of pipetting,

00:08:22.866 --> 00:08:24.500
you have higher variability,

00:08:24.933 --> 00:08:28.266
especially with cleanups
such as adaptor cleanups

00:08:28.433 --> 00:08:30.100
or your final sample yields.

00:08:30.766 --> 00:08:33.055
Often it requires
skilled pipetting.

00:08:33.166 --> 00:08:37.233
So what I mean by that
is transferring of viscous liquids,

00:08:37.566 --> 00:08:42.000
in-well tip mixing, or preventing
magnetic bead carryover

00:08:42.166 --> 00:08:45.366
while transferring
to supernatant off of a plate.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.933
Manual pipetting steps
can also contribute

00:08:49.066 --> 00:08:51.166
to user fatigue and injury.

00:08:52.366 --> 00:08:55.066
Manual processing
is highly error prone.

00:08:55.333 --> 00:08:59.733
Some examples would be sample
and/or index transfer mix-ups,

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:05.266
sample normalization errors,
Master Mix recipe calculation errors,

00:09:05.433 --> 00:09:07.900
and pipette volume setting errors.

00:09:08.033 --> 00:09:10.500
So basically setting
your pipette to the wrong volume

00:09:10.666 --> 00:09:12.766
affecting reaction chemistry,

00:09:14.266 --> 00:09:18.300
bead ratios, etc. that are important
for cleanup or size selection.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.433
In addition manual processing

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.000
provides limited throughput
and reduced data generation.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.866
Small labs,
or any labs for that matter,

00:09:28.966 --> 00:09:30.956
are always limited
in personnel resources –

00:09:31.066 --> 00:09:32.746
how many people
you have to get the work done,

00:09:32.866 --> 00:09:35.833
whether you have a small
or large amount of work,

00:09:35.966 --> 00:09:38.815
people always
are the limiting factor

00:09:38.933 --> 00:09:40.433
in how much you can get done.

00:09:41.833 --> 00:09:45.200
Often there is more effort spent
on routine sample processing

00:09:45.333 --> 00:09:49.000
versus
more value adding data analysis.

00:09:49.633 --> 00:09:51.803
So automation
can minimize or eliminate

00:09:51.933 --> 00:09:53.366
all of these challenges.

00:09:57.033 --> 00:09:58.533
So with that being said,

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:03.185
Beckman Coulter would like to think
that every lab would want to automate

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.266
but we hear from customers
they’re not ready to automate.

00:10:06.666 --> 00:10:08.633
And here
are several reasons that they give

00:10:08.966 --> 00:10:11.091
as to why they’re not ready
to make that jump

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.966
from manual processing
to automated processing.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:15.800
“We are too small.”

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:19.066
While automation reduces
the effort of routine processing

00:10:19.233 --> 00:10:23.000
it increases productivity even
with limited personnel resources.

00:10:23.266 --> 00:10:28.056
So it will allow your small lab
with a limited number of personnel

00:10:28.166 --> 00:10:30.966
to be able to do more work,
more effectively.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:34.865
And again, be able to focus on
the value adding data analysis work

00:10:34.966 --> 00:10:36.700
versus routine sample processing.

00:10:37.566 --> 00:10:39.896
Labs also say that “their
throughput is not high enough

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.400
to justify automating.”

00:10:41.733 --> 00:10:45.533
Well, throughput is not
the only reason for automation.

00:10:46.233 --> 00:10:47.913
The automation provides
more accurate

00:10:48.033 --> 00:10:49.600
and reproducible sample data,

00:10:49.733 --> 00:10:51.461
irrespective of sample numbers,

00:10:51.566 --> 00:10:54.433
and is scalable
to match the lab’s growing needs.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:59.533
Some labs feel that they “trust
their people more than machines.”

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.246
Biomek applications
are demonstrated and qualified

00:11:04.366 --> 00:11:06.258
with data to support
robust performance

00:11:06.366 --> 00:11:07.900
and I’ll talk
a little bit later

00:11:08.033 --> 00:11:11.666
about how our development process
works for our applications

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.700
and the types of data that
we generate to support

00:11:14.966 --> 00:11:17.500
those claims and
the performance of the application.

00:11:18.666 --> 00:11:19.643
“Budget.”

00:11:19.766 --> 00:11:23.300
Budgets are always limited whether
you’re a small lab or a large lab.

00:11:23.933 --> 00:11:25.246
So automation can provide

00:11:25.366 --> 00:11:27.461
significant savings
over manual processing

00:11:27.566 --> 00:11:29.933
through reduced labor
or overtime pay,

00:11:30.166 --> 00:11:32.600
reduced rework caused by human error,

00:11:33.066 --> 00:11:37.266
and reduced consumable usage via
efficient labware and tip handling.

00:11:38.033 --> 00:11:40.933
So encourage labs
to look at the big picture

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.538
of how automation
can fit into their workflow

00:11:43.666 --> 00:11:47.166
and provide an overall cost savings as
compared to manual processing.

00:11:48.166 --> 00:11:50.400
And lastly,
“We don’t have the expertise

00:11:50.533 --> 00:11:53.033
to operate and maintain
laboratory automation.”

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.661
So Biomek’s offer is
full-featured and easy to use

00:11:56.766 --> 00:11:59.196
which makes it the right solution
for either the novice

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:00.900
or experienced user.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.966
For the middle portion
of the presentation

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.633
we’re going to talk about
what Beckman Coulter does

00:12:12.866 --> 00:12:14.466
to develop an application.

00:12:14.966 --> 00:12:17.000
So we’re going to start
with market research

00:12:17.133 --> 00:12:19.633
followed by building
collaborative partnerships,

00:12:20.166 --> 00:12:23.333
scoping the requirements
and features of the application,

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:26.066
and then begin
a development process

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.166
where we develop
and optimize a method

00:12:28.333 --> 00:12:31.333
and test the method
with real reagents and generate data,

00:12:31.733 --> 00:12:35.166
and then produce content
such as tech notes,

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.733
application notes, posters,
webinars such as this

00:12:40.166 --> 00:12:43.500
(which go through an internal
and external review process).

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.735
That’s followed by
installations of the applications

00:12:46.866 --> 00:12:49.933
at the customer sites
and then post-installation support.

00:12:52.166 --> 00:12:53.333
So I’ll go over some details

00:12:53.466 --> 00:12:56.800
on each of those parts
of our development process.

00:12:58.033 --> 00:13:00.416
To start with
we have market research.

00:13:00.533 --> 00:13:01.891
We’re looking out into the field

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.000
to see
what applications are in demand

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.896
and we gather that feedback
from various places

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.800
whether that’s
trade shows, customers,

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:13.333
publications,
journal articles, etc.

00:13:13.466 --> 00:13:15.800
to see what people
are doing in their research.

00:13:16.733 --> 00:13:20.705
And we utilize that
to set a queue up to prioritize

00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:24.833
which applications we are going to
focus on developing.

00:13:27.233 --> 00:13:29.266
When we select
the application to develop

00:13:29.633 --> 00:13:31.488
we want to build
collaborative partnerships

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.200
with the chemistry vendors
and key research institutions.

00:13:35.633 --> 00:13:37.333
So there is great partnerships.

00:13:38.033 --> 00:13:43.566
We work to ensure that
the chemistry is being automated

00:13:43.766 --> 00:13:46.666
in the manner in which it was
intended to be run manually,

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.316
so working with
the chemistry vendors to ensure

00:13:49.433 --> 00:13:52.255
that the data quality
comes out automated

00:13:52.366 --> 00:13:54.866
the same as it does manually,
if not better.

00:13:55.866 --> 00:13:58.933
And we partner with key
research institutions – customers

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.766
who are either using
this chemistry already by hand

00:14:01.966 --> 00:14:05.533
or have an interest in automating
this chemistry in their lab.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.100
And we use that
as a real world case example

00:14:09.233 --> 00:14:12.666
for processing these samples
in a customer’s lab.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:14.626
So we generate data together

00:14:14.733 --> 00:14:16.600
and ensure
that through that partnership

00:14:16.766 --> 00:14:19.800
we have a robust
and reliable solution

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:22.900
to go off
to our broader customer base.

00:14:26.166 --> 00:14:28.366
So as those collaborations are made,

00:14:28.866 --> 00:14:32.200
the next step is to scope out
the requirements and features

00:14:32.333 --> 00:14:33.966
for the particular application.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.333
The goal is to develop
a “Template” method

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.755
that can be
readily deployed to customers.

00:14:38.866 --> 00:14:42.166
A “Template” method
is going to be a method

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:45.300
that covers the majority
of options and workflows

00:14:45.433 --> 00:14:47.400
that we expect to see
out in the field

00:14:47.533 --> 00:14:50.186
but still be flexible enough
to offer configuration

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.866
for user-specific needs
once it gets out into the field.

00:14:54.666 --> 00:14:57.833
We focus on things
such as throughput, capacity,

00:14:58.466 --> 00:14:59.800
walk-away time...

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.233
We consider various accessories

00:15:02.366 --> 00:15:04.300
and integrated devices
that are required

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.766
to perform
the various workflow steps.

00:15:08.233 --> 00:15:12.833
Workflow options such as
sample number, safe stop points,

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:18.401
any type of manual interactions
for flow cycle that might be needed –

00:15:18.533 --> 00:15:21.533
all those are considered
while scoping out the method

00:15:21.766 --> 00:15:24.733
as well as the
instrument platform selection.

00:15:28.666 --> 00:15:30.966
So I’ll talk about
our instrument platforms.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.696
We have multiple configurations

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:35.033
which provide
flexible throughput and capability

00:15:35.166 --> 00:15:37.300
to handle any NGS workflow.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:41.266
The Biomek 4000 is
our introductory benchtop model.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:44.433
It is sized just right for low
to medium throughput labs.

00:15:44.566 --> 00:15:46.233
Typically we develop applications

00:15:46.366 --> 00:15:49.900
anywhere from 1 to 24 samples
on this instrument.

00:15:50.233 --> 00:15:53.100
While it can handle
greater numbers of samples,

00:15:53.266 --> 00:15:59.133
it’s usually limited to that number
so that walk-away capability

00:15:59.266 --> 00:16:02.633
and efficiency
is still realized on that platform.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:07.353
It has a pod that has
interchangeable pipetting tools

00:16:07.466 --> 00:16:10.266
so there are single tools
and 8 channel pipetting heads.

00:16:10.566 --> 00:16:13.266
It also has
interchangeable grippers

00:16:13.466 --> 00:16:16.800
so you can have the ability
to move labware on the deck

00:16:16.933 --> 00:16:20.133
and not have them
be fixed in one spot.

00:16:21.033 --> 00:16:24.533
The deck layout below
is the typical example

00:16:24.766 --> 00:16:28.400
of our NGS setup
for the Biomek 4000.

00:16:28.566 --> 00:16:30.400
It includes an orbital shaker

00:16:31.066 --> 00:16:34.566
and static Peltier device
for keeping reagents chilled.

00:16:36.566 --> 00:16:39.336
The next platform is our Biomek NXP

00:16:39.466 --> 00:16:41.333
and that is going to be
our mid-sized unit.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:44.666
It’s capable of medium
to high throughput workflows

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:46.166
up to 96 samples.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:48.565
It has various pod options.

00:16:48.666 --> 00:16:53.328
It’s a single pod which can be
either a Span-8 or a multichannel pod –

00:16:53.433 --> 00:16:57.433
the multichannel having options
for either 96 or 384 well head.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.538
It also has the capability

00:16:59.666 --> 00:17:02.333
of enhanced
multi-selective tip pipetting,

00:17:02.566 --> 00:17:06.066
which is a multichannel head
that has the capability to pipette

00:17:06.233 --> 00:17:09.600
single rows, single columns,
or even individual wells.

00:17:10.433 --> 00:17:14.131
So it provides some flexibility
of a Span system

00:17:14.233 --> 00:17:17.333
while also providing the efficiency
of a multichannel head.

00:17:17.866 --> 00:17:19.976
There are approximately
16 deck positions

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:21.590
in a highly customizable deck

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:25.033
allowing an increase
in capacity over the Biomek 4000.

00:17:25.366 --> 00:17:29.433
And again, an example
of our typical NGS deck setup below –

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:32.721
with a static Peltier
in the back left corner,

00:17:32.833 --> 00:17:34.966
there’s an orbital shaker
in the back middle,

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:36.566
trash chute on the right,

00:17:36.833 --> 00:17:39.041
and the two positions
on the far left

00:17:39.166 --> 00:17:40.466
are stacking positions.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:41.866
That increases capacity

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.200
for additional tip storage
or plate storage.

00:17:48.333 --> 00:17:51.300
And lastly
we move into our Biomek FXP

00:17:51.466 --> 00:17:53.033
which is our largest platform.

00:17:55.966 --> 00:17:59.066
It’s a fitting system for high
throughput or complex workflows

00:17:59.333 --> 00:18:01.366
up to 96 samples or more.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.700
I should stress that
the amount of throughput

00:18:04.833 --> 00:18:06.400
you have, or size of your lab,

00:18:06.633 --> 00:18:09.091
doesn’t necessarily
dictate the size of the instrument

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.900
that would be right for you.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:17.266
If the workflow requirements
for your particular application

00:18:17.433 --> 00:18:21.125
require a lot of cleanups
and a lot of enzyme transfers

00:18:21.233 --> 00:18:24.500
then having a dual pod system,
regardless of throughput

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.633
or total capacity,
is still beneficial to your lab.

00:18:30.766 --> 00:18:34.666
So the Biomek FXP can be configured
with a combination of two pods

00:18:35.766 --> 00:18:38.120
and multichannel,
96 or 384 well head

00:18:38.233 --> 00:18:39.800
as well as the Span-8 pod.

00:18:40.033 --> 00:18:43.933
You can have two multichannels,
a multichannel and a Span –

00:18:44.466 --> 00:18:45.900
any option of those.

00:18:46.466 --> 00:18:48.026
And the deck is highly configurable.

00:18:48.133 --> 00:18:51.151
It offers a larger deck
than the NX or the Biomek 4000

00:18:51.266 --> 00:18:53.533
with approximately
20 deck positions.

00:18:54.466 --> 00:18:57.600
The example NGS deck layout
that we have below

00:18:57.733 --> 00:18:59.513
includes the integrated T-Robot

00:18:59.633 --> 00:19:01.766
which is our integrated
thermal cycler on the left.

00:19:02.233 --> 00:19:06.000
There is wash station
in the back row on the left

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.866
which allows for reuse
of tips after transfers,

00:19:10.033 --> 00:19:11.066
an orbital shaker,

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.900
and then also
a static Peltier device

00:19:14.033 --> 00:19:15.800
to keep
reagents chilled as well.

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:21.266
Continuing on to scoping options

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:23.841
and things that are considered
when developing the method,

00:19:23.966 --> 00:19:27.066
we have options
for labware selection,

00:19:27.266 --> 00:19:28.500
heating and cooling,

00:19:28.666 --> 00:19:31.566
orbital shaking and tip mixing,
and tip utilization.

00:19:32.866 --> 00:19:35.955
So labware selection
could be tubes versus plates.

00:19:36.066 --> 00:19:38.266
What's the right labware
for your application?

00:19:38.433 --> 00:19:41.900
It could be based on sample number,
dead volume in the tube,

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:46.233
what capacity
you need for your sample...

00:19:46.633 --> 00:19:48.766
We have options
for reagent reservoirs

00:19:49.133 --> 00:19:51.830
whether it’s a full plate reservoir...
We have modular reservoirs

00:19:51.933 --> 00:19:54.266
as shown
in the center picture below

00:19:54.433 --> 00:19:57.125
which can hold
different volumes of reagents

00:19:57.233 --> 00:20:00.806
based on the amount
needed for each application.

00:20:00.933 --> 00:20:03.066
The right modular reservoir

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:05.900
is matched to the volume
to reduce dead volume.

00:20:07.333 --> 00:20:11.400
There is a necessary
magnetic plate for bead separation

00:20:11.766 --> 00:20:14.000
with the example picture
on the bottom left there.

00:20:14.133 --> 00:20:17.458
And then thermal cycler lids
might be another labware option

00:20:17.566 --> 00:20:21.700
that’s needed with integrated
thermal cycling on your deck.

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:27.366
There’s consideration
for tip mixing versus shaking.

00:20:28.466 --> 00:20:31.300
Orbital shaking delivers fast,
efficient plate mixing,

00:20:31.466 --> 00:20:35.200
it reduces tip usage,
and provides uniform results.

00:20:35.433 --> 00:20:38.700
Whereas tip mixing
works well for larger volumes,

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:43.200
it prevents cross contamination
with labware that is at or near well capacity,

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:46.566
so if you have a well
that’s almost full, orbital shaking

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:49.496
isn’t the right option
for that particular mix step.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:53.000
So tip mixing would be deployed
for those purposes.

00:20:53.566 --> 00:20:55.966
And tip mixing can also
access tubes or labware

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.066
that are not compatible

00:20:58.433 --> 00:21:03.733
with an orbital shaker deck,
like a 6x4 aluminum tube rack

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:07.633
that will not fit
on your orbital shaker.

00:21:10.166 --> 00:21:11.433
Heating and cooling options.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:15.400
Peltiers are easy to use.

00:21:15.533 --> 00:21:17.986
They heat and cool.
There’s various adaptors

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:21.185
that can be fit
onto the surface of the Peltier

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.856
to accommodate various labwares –

00:21:22.966 --> 00:21:25.733
so round bottom wells, flat bottom,

00:21:26.366 --> 00:21:28.266
PCR adaptors, etc.

00:21:28.966 --> 00:21:31.926
It’s an open position
so there’s no plate sealing,

00:21:32.033 --> 00:21:33.600
so considerations have to be made

00:21:33.733 --> 00:21:36.566
for higher temperatures
and evaporation.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.726
There’s a slower ramping and
cooling speed thermal cyclers have

00:21:40.833 --> 00:21:43.030
and it’s not ideal
for all applications

00:21:43.133 --> 00:21:46.100
such as high temperatures,
because of evaporation issues.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:50.566
Thermal cycling can be
integrated on all of our platforms –

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:53.033
Biomek 4000, NXP, or FXP.

00:21:53.366 --> 00:21:56.255
And the thermal cyclers
provide a true walk-away solution

00:21:56.366 --> 00:22:04.133
not having to interact with the method
for every incubation that you need to perform.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:07.196
And if you’re familiar with them,
of the longer applications,

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:10.100
there’s multiple incubations
that take place

00:22:10.733 --> 00:22:13.960
which would require
multiple user interventions

00:22:14.066 --> 00:22:16.100
for off-deck thermal cycling.

00:22:16.233 --> 00:22:18.556
So this allows the instrument

00:22:18.666 --> 00:22:21.095
to move the plate automatically
through the thermal cycler

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:23.666
and continue the method
without any user intervention.

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:29.233
The plate sealing lids
prevent evaporation

00:22:29.533 --> 00:22:34.366
and then the T-Robots are compatible
with commonly used PCR labware.

00:22:34.766 --> 00:22:36.991
And we continue to do
development work

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:39.200
to integrate
additional manufacturers

00:22:39.566 --> 00:22:42.000
of thermal cycling
components as well.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.633
So tip selection,
choosing the tip type is important

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:50.666
depending on the type of reagent
you’re transferring

00:22:51.366 --> 00:22:53.300
to the type of labware
you’re trying to access.

00:22:53.433 --> 00:22:55.566
So our tips come
in various volume ranges

00:22:56.033 --> 00:22:58.418
from 20 microliters
up to 1,000 microliters.

00:22:58.533 --> 00:23:00.900
There are sterile barrier
or non-barrier options.

00:23:01.033 --> 00:23:04.233
And we also have conductive
and non-conductive tips

00:23:04.366 --> 00:23:06.400
which are used
for liquid level sensing.

00:23:07.066 --> 00:23:10.333
So tube access is an important
part of the selection.

00:23:10.466 --> 00:23:12.300
The P20 tip is shorter,

00:23:12.433 --> 00:23:15.533
it has higher volume without
the barrier than 20 microliters

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:19.033
and this might be the right option
for shallow microtiter plate.

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:21.600
Whereas a P50 is a longer tip.

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:22.780
It’s more slender

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:26.500
and it would work more effectively
with a deep well plate.

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:32.566
So after the labware
and the deck layout

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:34.033
and all the accessories

00:23:34.166 --> 00:23:37.766
and access devices that
are selected for the application,

00:23:38.333 --> 00:23:41.096
we then start looking
at method workflow options.

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:42.581
If you’re familiar
with our methods

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:45.800
you’re familiar
with the user interface that we have

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:47.825
at the start of the run.

00:23:47.933 --> 00:23:49.433
If you’re not familiar
with our methods

00:23:49.566 --> 00:23:51.633
all of our methods
have a user interface.

00:23:51.933 --> 00:23:56.533
And what the user interface does
is provides all the option settings

00:23:57.066 --> 00:23:59.400
that are relevant
to the specific applications.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.633
And it’s going to have things

00:24:01.766 --> 00:24:04.700
such as options for on
or off-deck thermal cycling,

00:24:04.966 --> 00:24:06.500
options for training modes

00:24:06.633 --> 00:24:09.366
which allows you
to quickly run through a method

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.086
without the lengthy incubations.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:12.900
That can be used for training,

00:24:13.133 --> 00:24:17.700
or optimization
and testing in your laboratory.

00:24:18.466 --> 00:24:19.333
It includes options

00:24:19.466 --> 00:24:22.531
for all relevant
application-specific options.

00:24:22.633 --> 00:24:27.066
That could be, in this case,
you have the Swift Biosciences method.

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:30.733
There’s sample input options
and fragment size options

00:24:30.933 --> 00:24:34.200
as well as which part of the method
that you’re wishing to run.

00:24:34.333 --> 00:24:38.066
So in this case PCR-Free portion
of the method was selected

00:24:38.266 --> 00:24:42.066
with various index
adaptor transfer options.

00:24:43.733 --> 00:24:47.033
All the options
are validated for proper selection

00:24:47.733 --> 00:24:50.733
to ensure that correct inputs
are made by the user

00:24:50.866 --> 00:24:54.333
and will not generate errors
upon execution of the run.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:58.133
And there’s also
safe stopping points built in

00:24:58.666 --> 00:25:01.736
to these options
to allow safe stopping points,

00:25:01.866 --> 00:25:03.960
matching the manufacturer’s protocol

00:25:04.066 --> 00:25:08.100
ability to safely store
your samples at break points.

00:25:11.233 --> 00:25:14.133
Safe stopping points
for pre and post PCR –

00:25:15.066 --> 00:25:18.733
being able to stop the method
and then transfer your samples

00:25:18.866 --> 00:25:21.690
to a different instrument
if that happens to be the way

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:25.533
that your lab's
quality management system dictates.

00:25:26.533 --> 00:25:28.900
It allows for
off-deck incubations

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:31.700
and safe stop points
allow for recovery

00:25:31.833 --> 00:25:34.620
by being able to perform
portions of the method

00:25:34.733 --> 00:25:37.900
or go back in the workflow
to a previous point

00:25:38.466 --> 00:25:39.766
and continue from there.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.966
And lastly, other considerations
we have on the deck

00:25:46.133 --> 00:25:49.366
for chemistry-specific issues.

00:25:50.033 --> 00:25:53.466
There can be multiple issues,
but some examples that we have here

00:25:53.633 --> 00:25:56.546
are considerations
for sodium hydroxide.

00:25:56.666 --> 00:26:00.100
So certain reactions are dependent

00:26:00.233 --> 00:26:02.933
on the sodium hydroxide
to be prepared fresh

00:26:03.066 --> 00:26:06.333
so we have
built-in considerations for that,

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:10.400
where prompts are presented to the user
at the right time in the method

00:26:10.533 --> 00:26:13.500
to present the deck
with that particular reagent.

00:26:14.266 --> 00:26:17.700
For ethanol we have considerations
over evaporation

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:21.696
and water adhesion into the ethanol.

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:25.000
So we want to ensure
that the depth volume

00:26:25.133 --> 00:26:27.626
is correct for ethanol
depending on the length of the run.

00:26:27.733 --> 00:26:28.751
Walk-away is great

00:26:28.866 --> 00:26:30.986
but if you have ethanol
on your deck for an entire day

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:33.168
then we definitely
have to have considerations

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:34.733
for those types of reagents.

00:26:37.133 --> 00:26:40.965
So after all those considerations
are made and scoping is set out

00:26:41.066 --> 00:26:44.100
we begin our development
and optimization process.

00:26:44.266 --> 00:26:48.133
So template methods are written
to follow manufacturer’s protocol

00:26:48.333 --> 00:26:49.600
and then we hit the lab.

00:26:49.733 --> 00:26:51.866
We have a facility
here in Indianapolis

00:26:52.033 --> 00:26:55.533
with multiple instruments
on all of our platforms.

00:26:55.766 --> 00:26:58.033
We use live chemistry

00:26:58.366 --> 00:27:02.066
and the ultimate goal
is to generate data with the kits

00:27:02.366 --> 00:27:04.600
that customers
are actually going to use.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.496
So to start
we do thorough dry testing

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:09.171
and logistical movement testing.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.700
We make sure
that the method structure is sound

00:27:11.933 --> 00:27:14.500
that the labware
is moving where it’s supposed to be.

00:27:14.866 --> 00:27:17.733
Pipetting is occurring
in the well at the right depth

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:20.133
whether that's from the bottom
or the top of the plate.

00:27:21.233 --> 00:27:24.300
When that all checks out,
we move into like-reagent testing

00:27:24.566 --> 00:27:26.533
and what that is, is using

00:27:29.366 --> 00:27:34.866
ethanol or glycerol solutions
to mimic enzyme transfers.

00:27:35.966 --> 00:27:38.166
We are the manufacturer
of our bead chemistry

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:42.300
that you see in all the NGS kits,
so we have the ability to test

00:27:43.033 --> 00:27:47.933
bead dispensing with an accuracy
of the bead ratios in house

00:27:48.166 --> 00:27:49.665
with our own chemistry.

00:27:49.766 --> 00:27:52.700
So we use
real chemistry when possible

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:55.566
during the like-reagent
testing and optimization.

00:27:57.266 --> 00:27:59.700
And then we go
into optimizing and calibrating

00:27:59.866 --> 00:28:03.533
pipetting specific to each
application or specific transfer.

00:28:03.733 --> 00:28:06.466
So again with bead cleanups,

00:28:06.933 --> 00:28:10.805
size selection, volume transfers,
and ratios are critical,

00:28:10.933 --> 00:28:12.996
same for any type of reaction setup.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:15.265
The balance
of the chemistry is critical

00:28:15.366 --> 00:28:18.356
to the optimal performance
of the application.

00:28:18.466 --> 00:28:21.766
So each of our
pipetting techniques can be dialed in

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:28.233
to very accurate volume transfers
across the range of the pipette tip.

00:28:29.066 --> 00:28:33.400
So we spend time to make sure that
that’s set up as accurately as possible

00:28:33.633 --> 00:28:36.100
to generate
the most robust results.

00:28:37.933 --> 00:28:40.975
Following the optimization, we move
into our live chemistry testing.

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:44.100
So we’ve ensured
that the method is sound,

00:28:44.233 --> 00:28:47.200
our pipetting optimization
is up to spec,

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:50.650
and then we actually run real kits

00:28:50.766 --> 00:28:53.366
with real chemistry
with real samples.

00:28:54.366 --> 00:28:56.433
If needed, after data is generated,

00:28:56.566 --> 00:28:59.133
we continue to do optimization

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:03.066
that we sometimes don't see
until we use the actual chemistry.

00:29:03.366 --> 00:29:06.081
And we continue
to fine-tune the method

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:08.400
until we have
the results that we’re looking for.

00:29:08.666 --> 00:29:10.131
So we determine those results

00:29:10.233 --> 00:29:13.200
based on generation of quantitative
and qualitative data

00:29:13.333 --> 00:29:16.866
such as bioanalyzer tracing,
qPCR data,

00:29:17.166 --> 00:29:21.000
and then downstream into the
sequencing data and sequencing metrics.

00:29:25.633 --> 00:29:29.231
So the next part of our process
is content generation and review

00:29:29.333 --> 00:29:32.506
and I’m going to hand off
to my colleague Zach Smith

00:29:32.633 --> 00:29:35.340
and he is going to continue
with our development process

00:29:35.466 --> 00:29:36.766
from this point forward.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:39.186
Thank you very much, David.
Thank you everyone

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:40.800
for sticking with us for this long.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.160
David’s done
a pretty good job so far

00:29:43.266 --> 00:29:45.500
and I’m hoping
to pick up where he left off.

00:29:46.266 --> 00:29:48.776
So content generation
and review process:

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:51.976
that’s where we generate
the technical and application notes

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:54.056
to demonstrate
the method capabilities

00:29:54.166 --> 00:29:55.900
and the robustness
of our application.

00:29:56.833 --> 00:30:00.755
The first thing we’ll do is
we will have the chemistry vendor

00:30:00.866 --> 00:30:04.865
go through our data, either our
QC traces from the bioanalyzer,

00:30:04.966 --> 00:30:08.606
qPCR results,
or our sequencing metrics

00:30:08.733 --> 00:30:11.171
and review those and make sure
their chemistry vendor

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:13.000
is content with the results.

00:30:13.333 --> 00:30:16.846
As David said, we have
a facility here in Indianapolis

00:30:16.966 --> 00:30:18.661
that is fully equipped
for this work.

00:30:18.766 --> 00:30:20.556
We have two sequencers in house,

00:30:20.666 --> 00:30:22.666
an aluminum MiSeq and NextSeq 500,

00:30:23.066 --> 00:30:25.833
that we can use to generate
the sequencing data we need.

00:30:26.433 --> 00:30:28.300
If we need more data

00:30:28.433 --> 00:30:29.966
or we have worked
on a couple of projects

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:31.766
that required HiSeq data

00:30:32.133 --> 00:30:35.000
we can certainly
work with the vendors

00:30:35.133 --> 00:30:38.600
or we send those out
for additional data analysis.

00:30:39.466 --> 00:30:42.600
And then we will work
the bioinformatics groups associated

00:30:42.733 --> 00:30:45.066
with the reagent vendors

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:49.933
to analyze those data
and interpret them properly.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:55.900
The data must meet
acceptable metrics per yield,

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:58.000
the sizing must be accurate

00:30:58.166 --> 00:31:00.666
and the sequencing metrics –
such as the percent

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:04.433
rRNA contamination,
if you’re working with an RNA method

00:31:04.633 --> 00:31:10.333
or the percent on target read
for a capture method –

00:31:10.833 --> 00:31:13.540
must be consistent
with what the reagent vendor

00:31:13.666 --> 00:31:15.400
is seeing in the manual prep.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:19.300
The other thing we look for
is evidence of cross examination.

00:31:19.466 --> 00:31:23.400
So our NGS methods are working
with all the plates, typically,

00:31:23.666 --> 00:31:25.936
which helps feed
the workflow along

00:31:26.066 --> 00:31:28.666
because then you’re not
having to seal and unseal plates.

00:31:28.866 --> 00:31:31.300
But we do realize that
there’s a possibility, I mean,

00:31:31.433 --> 00:31:34.258
this represents
a risk for cross contamination

00:31:34.366 --> 00:31:37.756
so it’s something we’re looking for
in the sequencing data.

00:31:37.866 --> 00:31:39.633
And it is something,

00:31:40.333 --> 00:31:42.366
because we’re looking for it
in the sequencing data,

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.000
you can see levels
that are not detectable

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:49.033
on bioanalyzer
or other fragment analysis traces.

00:31:49.266 --> 00:31:53.600
And so we feel like we really do go
one step beyond what’s typical

00:31:53.833 --> 00:31:56.666
in proving that
our automation solutions are sound

00:31:56.833 --> 00:32:00.200
and you can trust the data
that’s being generated.

00:32:01.833 --> 00:32:03.633
So once that review process is done

00:32:03.766 --> 00:32:07.020
then we will generate content
for public distribution

00:32:07.133 --> 00:32:10.633
like this webinar that you’ve
all kindly decided to show up for.

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:15.000
And then we’ll move on
to application installation.

00:32:15.133 --> 00:32:19.866
So we realized that, while we have
instrument configurations

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:26.810
that we generally
suggest for NGS applications,

00:32:26.933 --> 00:32:28.995
that our customers
are working with so much more

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:30.800
because our liquid handlers

00:32:30.966 --> 00:32:34.433
really are application agnostic
and they’re chemistry agnostic.

00:32:34.966 --> 00:32:37.433
And so you might be doing
NGS applications;

00:32:37.566 --> 00:32:39.533
you might be doing
some cellular or protein work,

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:44.233
and so we don’t force a customer
to buy a specific configuration

00:32:45.266 --> 00:32:46.500
for their instrument.

00:32:46.633 --> 00:32:50.200
So we recognize that the applications
therefore that we develop

00:32:50.333 --> 00:32:52.233
need to be flexible in their design

00:32:52.466 --> 00:32:54.533
and that they
can be configured properly.

00:32:54.666 --> 00:32:57.466
During the course
of application installation

00:32:57.700 --> 00:33:00.166
our field application specialists
will go through

00:33:00.333 --> 00:33:03.533
and they will implement
the method on your instrument.

00:33:03.866 --> 00:33:09.866
And they will perform a whole range
of on-site pipetting optimizations

00:33:10.033 --> 00:33:15.420
and on-site testing, frequently
going through a logistical test

00:33:15.533 --> 00:33:17.266
to make sure
the labware is moving properly,

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:19.333
that your stack offsets are correct...

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:21.700
And then they’ll go
through some pipetting testing

00:33:21.833 --> 00:33:23.466
and go through a complete water run,

00:33:23.666 --> 00:33:25.600
typically while they’re at a customer site,

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:29.600
so that you can verify
that pipetting is on target.

00:33:29.833 --> 00:33:33.633
And then after
the application is installed

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:35.333
we have an entire group

00:33:35.466 --> 00:33:37.900
that provides tech support
for these applications

00:33:38.133 --> 00:33:42.366
and other groups that provide
service support for the hardware.

00:33:42.566 --> 00:33:44.066
And then
we have another third group

00:33:44.233 --> 00:33:46.533
that provides training
and host user group meetings

00:33:46.733 --> 00:33:48.033
for our customers.

00:33:48.966 --> 00:33:50.733
So the reason why we do this –

00:33:50.966 --> 00:33:52.600
it’s not like
we’re just going to through

00:33:52.733 --> 00:33:54.600
and air drop in
an application into your site

00:33:54.766 --> 00:33:56.600
and expect you
to just know how to use it.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:34:01.733
We do backstop our applications with
a very extensive group of people

00:34:01.866 --> 00:34:05.066
that do various varieties
of support for our customers.

00:34:08.266 --> 00:34:11.766
So, there’s some
additional software features

00:34:11.933 --> 00:34:15.166
beyond Biomek software
that I wanted to touch on

00:34:15.333 --> 00:34:16.900
during the course
of this presentation.

00:34:17.066 --> 00:34:19.533
Both of which are fairly new,

00:34:19.666 --> 00:34:21.433
Biomek Method Launcher
being the newest.

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:24.766
But these are software packages
that we have developed

00:34:24.933 --> 00:34:30.066
because we feel like we can offer
an improved user experience

00:34:30.266 --> 00:34:31.766
and better data management

00:34:31.966 --> 00:34:34.933
and handling during the course
of the method being run.

00:34:40.666 --> 00:34:42.100
During the course of an average day

00:34:42.233 --> 00:34:44.866
you’ll come to your Biomek and
you’ll say, “I want to run a method.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:47.300
Well, are we sure
we picked the right project

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.900
and am I working with the most
recent version of my method?”

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:52.733
Or usually what we find out

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:56.100
is, “Our normal Biomek method operator
is on vacation

00:34:56.233 --> 00:34:57.766
and the PI wants me to run an assay

00:34:57.966 --> 00:34:59.633
and I really need
some help with this.”

00:35:00.166 --> 00:35:03.133
So, for those reasons
and several others –

00:35:03.333 --> 00:35:06.433
and by the way, this is what our
standard user interface looks like.

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:10.900
So if you were to open up
the editor on your controller

00:35:11.066 --> 00:35:14.600
this is what you would see.
It’s fairly straightforward to use.

00:35:14.766 --> 00:35:16.300
As far as programming is concerned

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:18.966
the UI gives you
a GUI faced interface

00:35:19.100 --> 00:35:20.500
for building methods

00:35:20.633 --> 00:35:25.133
and building out software solutions
if you want to do that.

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:29.433
Alternatively, you can use
Biomek Method Launcher

00:35:29.633 --> 00:35:32.066
to launch your application
that’s been installed already.

00:35:32.233 --> 00:35:34.800
So Biomek Method Launcher
is a new product.

00:35:35.033 --> 00:35:36.700
It has two components to it.

00:35:36.866 --> 00:35:39.233
One’s the launcher interface,
which you’re seeing here,

00:35:39.433 --> 00:35:41.433
and the other one
is Guided Labware Setup.

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:44.233
Those are
the two primary components here.

00:35:44.433 --> 00:35:47.833
So the launcher allows you
to interact with your instrument

00:35:47.966 --> 00:35:49.733
without actually
running the editor software

00:35:49.866 --> 00:35:53.066
so you would just go in
and begin Biomek Method Launcher

00:35:53.333 --> 00:35:56.266
and your applications would be
listed out there on the launcher

00:35:56.466 --> 00:35:59.200
and there would be icons that
allow you to select the right one.

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:03.900
You can also perform
manual control on the instrument

00:36:04.100 --> 00:36:06.200
through the launcher as well,
just by clicking down

00:36:06.433 --> 00:36:08.733
on the manual control tab

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:10.533
and it'll bring up a whole
a variety of options

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:12.600
that you can use
to run the instrument.

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:16.166
Once you’ve selected your method,

00:36:16.366 --> 00:36:19.933
say for example, you want to run
Illumina TruSight HLA version 2,

00:36:20.600 --> 00:36:23.200
an html based user interface
would pop up

00:36:23.333 --> 00:36:26.086
and would give you the options
you need to run the method.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:27.966
How many samples
you want to process

00:36:28.166 --> 00:36:30.766
and which components of the method
that you want to run.

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:35.000
We built these UIs in such a way

00:36:35.166 --> 00:36:38.100
that they’re easy to operate
and should be fairly straightforward

00:36:38.233 --> 00:36:40.366
for any operator to employ.

00:36:42.333 --> 00:36:45.166
The other component
of Biomek Method Launcher

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:47.433
is a new step
called Guided Labware Setup.

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:49.900
So Guided Labware Setup
will step you through

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:55.126
on a plate-by-plate,
tip box-by-tip box process

00:36:55.233 --> 00:36:59.866
to set the deck up in such
a way that it’s properly set up.

00:37:00.066 --> 00:37:03.133
And one of the things it really
does nicely is that it will go through

00:37:03.266 --> 00:37:05.400
and will show you a side view
of stacks of labware,

00:37:05.533 --> 00:37:07.966
so you can see that
on the right hand side of your screen.

00:37:09.033 --> 00:37:11.766
And for reagents
that are deployed in troughs

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:14.666
or you have master mixes
that you need to create –

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:16.833
all those setup instructions
are included

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.100
in the individual
labware descriptions.

00:37:19.366 --> 00:37:22.866
Additionally, you can print off
the Guided Labware Setup as a PDF

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.633
and you can take those setup
instructions back to your bench

00:37:25.966 --> 00:37:28.600
or you can go and make up
your master mixes.

00:37:28.933 --> 00:37:33.900
The other thing
you can do with those PDFs

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:37.366
is those PDFs can be uploaded
to a LIM system, for example,

00:37:37.533 --> 00:37:40.933
or copied into a lab notebook
to show the user,

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:43.500
“This is what I’ve done
on this particular day.”

00:37:43.700 --> 00:37:48.700
It’s a really powerful interface
and I like it quite a lot actually.

00:37:50.433 --> 00:37:52.900
So once you get beyond
Guided Labware Setup, then,

00:37:53.033 --> 00:37:54.633
during the course
of the method running

00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:57.966
this is what the Run Time Interface

00:37:58.166 --> 00:38:00.433
would look like
for Biomek Method Launcher.

00:38:00.966 --> 00:38:04.533
So on the left,
you’ll see the method screen

00:38:04.733 --> 00:38:07.433
where it will go through and give
you the progress bar on the top

00:38:07.633 --> 00:38:11.466
that will show you how long
the method is going to continue.

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:13.066
So this answers
the vital question of:

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:14.833
Can I go and get a cup of coffee?

00:38:14.966 --> 00:38:16.406
Do I have time
to go to the bathroom?

00:38:16.533 --> 00:38:18.533
Or is it time
to interact with the instrument?

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:19.660
And then it will go through

00:38:19.766 --> 00:38:21.326
and will offer you
a series of milestones

00:38:21.433 --> 00:38:23.253
to let you know
what process it’s working on

00:38:23.366 --> 00:38:25.733
and how long that individual
process is going to take.

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:28.880
If you flip over
to the method view over here

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:31.233
on the right hand side
of your screen,

00:38:31.766 --> 00:38:34.466
it will actually go through
and show you the Biomek steps

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:36.166
that it’s actually looking through.

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:38.366
And if you go
into the labware dialog

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:43.600
which is on the right (bottom right)
of each of those screens

00:38:43.866 --> 00:38:45.956
you can actually click
and view the volumes

00:38:46.066 --> 00:38:48.773
on every individual piece of labware
as the method is progressing.

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.866
So you can monitor
its running in real time.

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:58.000
Oh yes, data handling.

00:39:01.166 --> 00:39:03.586
Automation methods
can generate a large number of data

00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:05.033
that will need to be tracked.

00:39:05.566 --> 00:39:09.133
And just one well,
for example, of this one plate

00:39:09.266 --> 00:39:12.333
we could track
what the sample ID is,

00:39:12.466 --> 00:39:15.566
what the project ID is,
what the PI is we’re working for

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:17.933
on this particular project,

00:39:18.066 --> 00:39:21.526
what species it is, the name
of the technician running the assay,

00:39:21.633 --> 00:39:24.233
concentrations, volumes,
the list goes on and on.

00:39:24.500 --> 00:39:28.926
And the issue gets exponentially
worse the more plates you process.

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:31.333
It leads to a lot of data
that needs to be handled.

00:39:33.500 --> 00:39:35.256
And of course it’s not just from...

00:39:35.366 --> 00:39:36.706
This data is piling up

00:39:36.833 --> 00:39:38.733
during the course
of numerous applications.

00:39:38.866 --> 00:39:43.166
So if you’re starting off
with NGS samples, either DNA or RNA,

00:39:43.300 --> 00:39:46.566
you’ll want to know where
those samples were sectioned from,

00:39:46.866 --> 00:39:48.860
any data that’s associated with them,

00:39:48.966 --> 00:39:51.500
including species or the volume
or the concentration,

00:39:51.966 --> 00:39:54.100
or maybe you’ll have to
quantify them yourself

00:39:54.233 --> 00:39:55.400
when you get them in.

00:39:55.533 --> 00:39:59.166
So one of the things you can do
is you can merge the detector data

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:01.766
and then track the data
and sample ID to the dilution plate

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:04.033
as you quantify
and normalize the DNA.

00:40:04.433 --> 00:40:06.946
Then there’s a matter
of doing your NGS library prep

00:40:07.066 --> 00:40:09.100
and so at that point,
you’ll want to track

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:11.666
what samples
are associated with what indices

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:14.166
so that you can do
multiplex samples later.

00:40:14.766 --> 00:40:16.760
And then you’ll want to export
some of that data into

00:40:16.866 --> 00:40:19.600
the Illumina Experiment Manager
for your sample sheet.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:23.860
And then also extend
that data to a LIM system

00:40:23.966 --> 00:40:26.466
if your lab has one
for a downstream analysis.

00:40:30.933 --> 00:40:36.466
So DART 2.0, stands for
Data Acquisition and Reporting Tool.

00:40:36.666 --> 00:40:42.433
It is a software package
that works with Biomek and SQL

00:40:42.600 --> 00:40:45.466
to generate a repository that will
track all of that information

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:47.033
on a per plate basis.

00:40:47.166 --> 00:40:48.500
So it can take reader data

00:40:48.633 --> 00:40:51.160
it will take data from data sets
that are generated

00:40:51.266 --> 00:40:54.333
as part of a normal operation
from Biomek software.

00:40:54.566 --> 00:40:59.333
And it can report them out
in other devices, a LIM system,

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:01.146
any web-enabled device

00:41:01.266 --> 00:41:03.733
that will allow you to monitor
the run in real time,

00:41:03.866 --> 00:41:06.666
or other terminals
inside or outside the lab

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:08.600
if you have that configured right.

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:18.080
There are several ways
you can access the data with DART.

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:22.326
So you can have the DART software
create labware reports

00:41:22.433 --> 00:41:26.533
that will automatically just
export into, say, Excel sheets

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:30.520
and then the report contains
an embedded link to the database

00:41:30.633 --> 00:41:33.000
so you can just click refresh
on your Excel sheet

00:41:33.166 --> 00:41:35.960
and then it will
automatically repopulate that sheet

00:41:36.066 --> 00:41:37.593
with the fresh data
from the report

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:39.866
if you’d ran
another assay for example.

00:41:40.333 --> 00:41:41.733
So that’s really convenient.

00:41:42.833 --> 00:41:45.626
The other thing you can do
is you can monitor runs in real time

00:41:45.733 --> 00:41:48.066
using the web interface.

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:50.466
So DART will report out
that the method is running,

00:41:50.600 --> 00:41:51.733
how long it’s been running...

00:41:51.933 --> 00:41:55.000
You can query
all the various sorts of labware

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:59.100
and find out what’s been done
and who has been doing the run.

00:42:03.033 --> 00:42:06.026
And you can use it
to generate all kinds of reports.

00:42:06.133 --> 00:42:10.726
So on the top left we’ve got
what the query builder looks like

00:42:10.833 --> 00:42:14.733
and you can basically,
if you’re building a report out,

00:42:14.866 --> 00:42:16.740
it’s just a matter of clicking

00:42:16.866 --> 00:42:18.766
which fields
you want to include in the report.

00:42:18.933 --> 00:42:23.266
And so above, for example,
I’ve got an illustration

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:27.333
where you’re using DART
to report data out

00:42:27.466 --> 00:42:29.046
to the Illumina Experiment Manager

00:42:29.166 --> 00:42:31.966
for sample sheet generation
including the sample

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:34.980
and which I5 and I7 indices

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:37.066
have been associated
with which library.

00:42:37.266 --> 00:42:38.846
And on the left on the bottom there

00:42:38.966 --> 00:42:42.096
we have the Library
Normalization Report from DART 2.0

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:46.133
where we’re going to be showing
what the starting concentration was,

00:42:46.266 --> 00:42:47.400
how much volume was moved,

00:42:47.533 --> 00:42:50.366
and what the normalization
concentration was afterwards.

00:42:50.833 --> 00:42:52.586
So it allows you a variety of ways

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:55.000
of getting access
to your data and storing it.

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:05.133
So finally I wanted to just go
over some of our application cards.

00:43:05.266 --> 00:43:08.406
So we’ve got a website
with a link you can see above

00:43:08.533 --> 00:43:10.800
info.beckmancoulter.com/NGS.

00:43:10.933 --> 00:43:15.033
And there you’ll find our current
NGS library preparation method card.

00:43:15.166 --> 00:43:19.466
So this method card will detail
what methods we’ve developed

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:22.233
and what applications
we’ve developed on which platforms.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:25.933
This list is continually growing

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:29.766
and we’re
