as many questions as time permits.

00:44:14.466 --> 00:44:16.566
And the first question is

00:44:16.766 --> 00:44:20.033
for the Biomeks
capable of the 384 format,

00:44:20.366 --> 00:44:22.253
is there a 384 well head?

00:44:22.366 --> 00:44:26.100
Or are you pipetting four times
with the 96 well head?

00:44:26.333 --> 00:44:31.366
And further, can the 96 head
pipette individual volume?

00:44:33.300 --> 00:44:35.733
Okay. I can answer that question.
This is David.

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:37.986
The answer is yes,

00:44:38.100 --> 00:44:43.200
the Biomeks do have options
for either a 96 or a 384 well head.

00:44:43.366 --> 00:44:45.820
As far as the second part
of the question,

00:44:45.933 --> 00:44:49.066
the heads cannot
pipette individual volumes

00:44:49.333 --> 00:44:52.700
as a 96 or 384 well transfer.

00:44:52.833 --> 00:44:56.966
However, we do have the enhanced
elected tip pipetting heads

00:44:57.100 --> 00:44:59.193
which are capable
of doing individual row,

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:01.733
column,
or individual well pipetting.

00:45:01.933 --> 00:45:06.526
So if you did have a 96
or 384 well labware format

00:45:06.633 --> 00:45:10.000
that required different volumes
in different wells

00:45:10.566 --> 00:45:13.566
you could use
the enhanced selective tip feature

00:45:13.700 --> 00:45:18.040
to either deliver
or aspirate off different volumes

00:45:18.166 --> 00:45:22.466
from different wells
within that 96 or 384 well labware.

00:45:25.233 --> 00:45:28.940
Okay. How long does it take
to implement an existing method

00:45:29.066 --> 00:45:32.266
with no modifications
versus one with modifications?

00:45:34.766 --> 00:45:36.666
Yeah. I can take that.

00:45:37.366 --> 00:45:40.633
So typically what we found
when we installed method

00:45:40.766 --> 00:45:42.393
is that usually
there will have to be

00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:46.806
some sort of modification
made to it when it’s deployed.

00:45:46.933 --> 00:45:49.526
So we will have a customer
who wants to change

00:45:49.633 --> 00:45:52.360
which PCR plate
is being used, for example,

00:45:52.466 --> 00:45:54.433
or they’ll have
some other requirements.

00:45:54.566 --> 00:45:55.900
Say, for example,

00:45:56.033 --> 00:45:58.686
in the case of labs that are
working in a clinical environment,

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:00.533
they don’t want to reuse tips

00:46:01.466 --> 00:46:05.366
so we will have to provide
a reconfiguration for that.

00:46:06.066 --> 00:46:07.796
If you had no modifications to make

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:10.253
it’s literally a matter
of importing the method

00:46:10.366 --> 00:46:14.100
into your Biomek Controller
which takes all of about 30 seconds

00:46:14.266 --> 00:46:17.766
and then mapping it
on to the customer deck

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:20.366
which again
does not take very much time.

00:46:20.533 --> 00:46:22.833
And then we will go
through the process

00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:26.413
of validating the pipetting
and doing the water test run.

00:46:26.533 --> 00:46:31.533
And that depends largely
on how complex the protocol is.

00:46:32.433 --> 00:46:34.766
So for TruSeq PCR-Free
with no modifications

00:46:34.900 --> 00:46:37.700
it would be approximately
two days with no changes.

00:46:37.966 --> 00:46:40.013
But it might be more than that,

00:46:40.133 --> 00:46:42.800
assuming that we needed
to make some modifications.

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:49.366
Can any of the Biomeks process
any kind of genomics kits?

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:55.146
Yes, our automation
is chemistry agnostic

00:46:55.266 --> 00:46:58.593
so we can provide
automation solutions for Illumina,

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:01.100
New England Bio Labs,
Rubicon, Swift...

00:47:01.566 --> 00:47:05.833
other vendors that may
or may not be more boutique.

00:47:08.333 --> 00:47:11.360
So we also have a variety
of extraction chemistries

00:47:11.466 --> 00:47:12.686
that we sell ourselves

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:15.760
including FormaPure, DNAdvance,
and RNAdvance

00:47:15.866 --> 00:47:18.220
and we can certainly get you
more information on those

00:47:18.333 --> 00:47:19.233
if you’re interested.

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:23.326
How do I get support
for developing methods

00:47:23.433 --> 00:47:24.500
not on your list?

00:47:24.666 --> 00:47:27.200
Do I need to wait
until your team provides them?

00:47:28.633 --> 00:47:29.666
I can take this one.

00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:33.566
So we do have
a pretty exhaustive list

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:37.400
of applications
that are demonstrated or qualified.

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:40.660
But if you happen
to have a requirement

00:47:40.766 --> 00:47:42.800
for an application
that’s not on the list,

00:47:42.933 --> 00:47:48.700
then we do have a field team
that covers the US, globally actually,

00:47:48.866 --> 00:47:51.233
to be able to handle
those specific needs

00:47:51.366 --> 00:47:52.826
and develop methods
out in the field.

00:47:52.933 --> 00:47:56.433
So a lot of times the voice of customer
and the market research

00:47:56.566 --> 00:48:00.333
that I talked about earlier
actually comes from our field team.

00:48:00.466 --> 00:48:02.600
So they’re out working
with the customers every day

00:48:02.766 --> 00:48:05.600
and method application development

00:48:05.766 --> 00:48:08.340
could actually start in the field
and it does quite often.

00:48:08.466 --> 00:48:11.800
So they will develop those methods

00:48:11.933 --> 00:48:15.080
then route them
through the internal group

00:48:15.200 --> 00:48:18.266
and allow us to kind of
add additional features,

00:48:19.333 --> 00:48:20.706
the UI options, etc.

00:48:20.833 --> 00:48:26.500
that will then be able
to be utilized by many labs.

00:48:26.733 --> 00:48:31.326
And in addition,
the collaborative support

00:48:31.433 --> 00:48:34.366
that we have for the in house
development of applications

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:37.226
extends out to methods
that were developed in the field.

00:48:37.333 --> 00:48:40.866
So if a customer wants
a particular vendor’s chemistry,

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:44.686
field application scientist,
develops it, then those vendors

00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:47.586
do partner and work closely with us

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:51.766
to ensure that customers’ results
are optimized

00:48:51.900 --> 00:48:54.533
for field developed methods as well.

00:48:56.500 --> 00:48:59.166
How often
do you add methods to your menu?

00:49:02.533 --> 00:49:03.733
It seems like weekly.

00:49:05.800 --> 00:49:09.866
Just in the last few weeks,
we’ve added five applications

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:14.166
to the repository that we
sourced the method card from.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:16.953
So our development process
is continuous

00:49:17.066 --> 00:49:18.800
and it’s mostly based
on market demand.

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:22.600
So we have a focus on providing
more solutions for small labs now

00:49:22.733 --> 00:49:25.333
so you’ll see an increase
in the cadence of Biomek 4000

00:49:25.466 --> 00:49:28.000
and Biomek NXP methods.

00:49:31.000 --> 00:49:34.066
What’s the pipetting
volume range for your system?

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:36.933
I can handle that one too.

00:49:37.633 --> 00:49:39.700
So for the Biomek 4000

00:49:39.833 --> 00:49:42.733
we can pipette anywhere
from 1 to 1,000 microliters

00:49:42.866 --> 00:49:46.833
depending on the tip that’s loaded
and the head that’s used

00:49:47.033 --> 00:49:50.000
for a 1 microliter transfer,
for a P20 tip,

00:49:50.133 --> 00:49:53.533
the precision
or the inaccuracy is 5 percent.

00:49:54.433 --> 00:49:57.066
For a P1000 tip,

00:49:57.233 --> 00:50:01.533
for a 1,000 microliter transfer,
it’s 2 percent inaccuracy.

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:05.266
For our Span-8 systems,

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:09.533
we can pipette accurately
down to half a microliter

00:50:09.800 --> 00:50:12.133
with an accuracy
of approximately 5 percent.

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:16.866
We do actually have
an application note around that

00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:18.820
but we can certainly get the people

00:50:18.933 --> 00:50:21.666
if they’re interested
in seeing some of the data.

00:50:24.166 --> 00:50:27.233
Is there any project to get
low retention tips from Beckman?

00:50:29.866 --> 00:50:31.466
I can answer that.

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:35.300
Basically, the plastic
that we use is always,

00:50:35.633 --> 00:50:37.686
I guess they call it,
virgin polystyrene.

00:50:37.800 --> 00:50:39.100
It’s not recycled.

00:50:39.933 --> 00:50:45.000
And the tips are all essentially
spec’ed out to be low retention.

00:50:46.033 --> 00:50:51.033
And a lot of the pipetting
optimization also increases

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:54.700
the recovery from samples
and liquids within the tips

00:50:54.866 --> 00:50:58.996
so for viscous liquid,
slower pipetting helps deliver

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:03.000
controlled delivery
of the viscous liquid

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:04.933
ensures optimal recovery.

00:51:05.500 --> 00:51:08.166
But again, all of our tips
are considered to be low retention.

00:51:10.066 --> 00:51:12.666
